English you have to know it if you want  to fit in this globalized world without  English you would not be able to  understand this very same video hi my  name is Luca from Luca procom and today  I'm going to talk about how I've learned  English and the key lessons I've learned  along this amazing journey nowadays I  use English every single day and it has  become second nature to me the things  were actually taught at the very  beginning when I used to attend school  and learn English as my first foreign  language actually things were really  difficult my pronunciation was atrocious  I got overwhelmed by all these phrasal  verbs and I got confused by tenses  I could not stand grammar and basically  I felt down every time and try to speak  and get my point across to make a long  story short it was a mess but then the  moment came when Susan my American  teacher came and started giving me  lessons the biggest lesson I've learned  from her is not about phrasal verbs is  not about tenses it's not about grammar  it was about attitude my entire attitude  towards the English language changed I  started seeing every single mistake I  made not as a failure but as an  opportunity at door that opened and that  made all the difference so whenever  you're learning a language you're  starting any language in the world  remember to write down things such as  growth happiness joy enjoyment because  these are the words that guide you and  will guide you through your learning all  those mistakes errors hesitations and  not being able to get my point across  they were all there for me to learn I  really liked the quote that says that  10% is what happens to you and 90% is  how you react to it and that's exactly  the attitude I had when I started and I  kept learning English along all these  years  when Susan and I started working  together on my English outside of the  class I realized another big thing that  I was not focused on marks I was not  focused on the performance I was focused  on the process I was enjoying the  process itself a language learner is  like a mountain climber the expert  climber doesn't focus so much on the  peak of the mountain doesn't think about  that he thinks about the process he  enjoys every step of the way and so  should a good language learner do he  should enjoy the process every step of  the way in thinking of how amazing it is  to climb towards the language peak when  that happens you don't focus on mistakes  you don't focus on the performance you  don't focus on the fact that you're  making or not making mistakes you're  focusing on the act of communicating and  there's a consequence of that you're  going to learn a ton that makes a huge  difference and most of us are focused on  performance because we're used to this  school system where we get marks and  we're words out of it but language  learning is not about that it's about  communication it's about life it's about  friends  it's about smiles it's about enjoying a  life  [Music]  if you speak a language fluently you  know you have experienced that Eureka  moment when you start enjoying the  language and you forget about learning  it you're just using it you're enjoying  it and to keep it with the climber  metaphor that's exactly it a climber  just instead of focusing on the peak of  the mountain turns back and every step  of the way  enjoys the view and every single step  you're higher you can get a better sight  of what is down there you can start from  the very beginning I remember that when  I started working with Susan what  changed is that yes I was still learning  English but I was using it I used to I  started watching videos I started  watching movies I started communicating  with her maybe sending a few messages  here and there I started enjoying the  language on top of learning it itself  and this is really important it will  keep you along the way you will keep  walking towards the mountain because  it's not just the idea of walking it's  also the idea of enjoying while you walk  while you climb when you get closer to  the peak this is not to say the things  will get super smooth you will stumble  sometimes you will stop you will run out  of breath and sometimes you will ask  yourself why the hell am i doing this in  the first place but then you should  remember Confucius words our greatest  glory is not in falling it's in rising  every time we fall  [Music]  so here we have it this is how I've  learned English and like any other  language English takes time to learn but  along the journey you have to keep in  mind the three things can make a  difference the first one is a positive  mindset you can see everything like  black or white but it's up to you to  decide the second thing that you have to  remember is to focus on the process  enjoy the process don't focus too much  on the results otherwise like a climber  you will constantly think about the peak  and not focus on the path in front of  you and the third you have to start  using the language as soon as you can on  top of learning that because that will  create momentum you will actually  realize how cool it is to use a language  to communicate with other human beings  because ultimately language learning  speaking language is about living and  enjoying and talking to people if you  want to know more about this I've  written an extensive article about how  I've learned English you can click on  the link in the description box you can  read and listen to the article like all  the other articles that I have on my  blog Lou Calipari lol calm and as always  thanks for watching  [Music]  you  [Music]  you
 

